WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:05.180
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:09.390
We are here to answer a simple question: what
is peer review?

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:15.330
Well, in science, making sure that the validity
of published results is beyond doubt is of

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:16.550
utmost importance.

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:22.440
To this end, many scientific journals and
conferences exist where researchers can submit

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:25.570
their findings in the form of a science paper.

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:30.930
As a condition of acceptance, these papers
shall undergo extensive scrutiny by typically

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:34.230
2 to 5 other scientists.

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:37.719
This refereeing process we call peer review.

00:00:37.719 --> 00:00:42.460
Single blind reviewing means that the names
of the reviewers are shrouded in mystery,

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:45.489
but the authors of the paper are known to
them.

00:00:45.489 --> 00:00:50.519
In double blind reviews, however, the papers
are anonymized, and none of the parties know

00:00:50.519 --> 00:00:52.260
the names of each other.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:56.789
These different kinds of blind reviews were
made to eliminate possible people-related

00:00:56.789 --> 00:00:57.789
biases.

00:00:57.789 --> 00:01:01.859
There is a lot of discussion whether they
do a good job at that or not, but this is

00:01:01.859 --> 00:01:03.199
what they are for.

00:01:03.199 --> 00:01:08.100
After the review, if the results are found
to be correct, and the reviews are favorable

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:14.610
enough, the paper is accepted and subsequently
published in a journal and/or presented at

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:15.840
a conference.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:22.070
Usually, the higher the prestige of a publication
venue is, the higher the likelihood of rejection,

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:27.200
which inevitably raises a big question: how
to choose the papers that are to be accepted?

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:32.830
As we are scientists, we have to try to ensure
that the peer review is a fair and consistent

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:33.830
process.

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:37.660
To measure if this is the case, the NIPS experiment
was born.

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:42.710
NIPS is one of the highest quality conferences
in machine learning with a remarkably low

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:47.180
acceptance ratio, which typically hovers below
25%.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:52.200
This is indeed remarkably low considering
the fact that many of the best research groups

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:55.240
in the world submit their finest works here.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:02:00.300
So here is the astute idea behind the NIPS
experiment: a large amount of papers would

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:05.780
be secretly disseminated to multiple committees,
they would review it without knowing about

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:12.049
each other, and we would have a look whether
they would accept or reject the same papers.

00:02:12.049 --> 00:02:16.280
Re-reviewing papers and see if the results
is the same, if you will.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:23.510
At a given prescribed acceptance ratio, there
was a disagreement for 57% of the papers.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:27.920
This means that one of the committees would
accept the paper and the other wouldn't, and

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:29.019
vice versa.

00:02:29.019 --> 00:02:34.310
Now, to put this number into perspective,
the mathematical model of a random committee

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:35.680
was put together.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:40.609
This means that the members of this committee
have no idea what they are doing, and as a

00:02:40.609 --> 00:02:46.700
review, they basically toss up a coin and
accept or reject the paper based on the result.

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:51.590
The calculations conclude that this random
committee would have this disagreement ratio

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:54.260
of about 77%.

00:02:54.260 --> 00:03:00.040
This is hardly something to be proud of: the
consistency of expert reviewers is significantly

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:05.299
closer to a coinflip than to a hypothetical
perfect review process.

00:03:05.299 --> 00:03:12.840
So, experts, 57% disagreement, Coinflip Committee,
77% disagreement.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:18.419
It is not as bad as the Coinflip Committee,
so the question naturally arises: where are

00:03:18.419 --> 00:03:19.550
the differences?

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:25.819
Well, it seems that the top 10% of the papers
are clearly accepted by both committees, the

00:03:25.819 --> 00:03:32.560
bottom 25% of the papers are clearly rejected,
this is the good news, and the bad news is

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:37.530
that anything between might as well be decided
with a cointoss.

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:42.790
If the consistency of peer review is subject
to maximization, we clearly have to do something

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:43.790
different.

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:49.109
Huge respect for the NIPS organizers for doing
this laborious experiment, for the reviewers

00:03:49.109 --> 00:03:54.340
who did a ton of extra work, and kudos for
the fact that the organizers were willing

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:57.359
to release such uncomfortable results.

00:03:57.359 --> 00:04:01.739
This is very important, and is the only way
of improving our processes.

00:04:01.739 --> 00:04:06.459
Hopefully, someday we shall have our revenge
over the Coinflip Committee.

00:04:06.459 --> 00:04:08.540
Can we do something about this?

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:10.230
What is a possible solution?

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:15.989
Well, of course, this is a large and difficult
problem for which I don't pretend to have

00:04:15.989 --> 00:04:21.720
any perfect solutions, but there is a really
interesting idea by a renowned professor about

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:25.580
crowdsourcing reviews that I found to be spectacular.

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:30.370
I'll leave the blog post in the comments section
both for this and the NIPS experiment, and

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:33.190
we shall have an entire episode about this
soon.

00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:34.190
Stay tuned!

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:38.090
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

